\section{Checkpoint Resume Artifact}
\label{app:resume_artifact}

The adaptive model for seed\,42 in Table~\ref{tab:main} was trained with a checkpoint-resumed run whose first ${\sim}$1{,}000 steps used different hyperparameters (learning rate warmup, gradient accumulation configuration) than seeds 123 and 456, which were trained from scratch.
This resume artifact inflated the in-distribution effect size from $\Delta{=}{-}0.011$ (from-scratch 3-seed mean) to $\Delta{=}{-}0.043$ (mixed 3-seed mean including resume seed\,42).

The artifact disproportionately affected out-of-distribution evaluation:

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Eval domain} & \textbf{Resume (seed\,42)} & \textbf{From-scratch (3-seed)} & \textbf{Direction} \\
\midrule
FineWeb (in-dist) & $-$0.041 & $-$0.011 & same \\
OpenWebText (OOD) & $+$0.058 & $-$0.004 & reversed \\
Wikipedia (OOD)   & $+$0.137 & $-$0.002 & reversed \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The resume model showed apparent distribution matching (OOD degradation), which was the basis for the ``distribution matching'' finding in earlier analyses.
From-scratch retraining eliminates this pattern: all three evaluation domains show small, directionally consistent improvements, providing no evidence of distribution matching.
This underscores the importance of controlling for training artifacts when interpreting small effect sizes across evaluation domains.

